},"purpose":"Challenge f_7ea2811d: Verify if C:\\dllhost.exe or C:\\windows\\system32\\dllhost\\ directory appear in baseline XP image file listing — could be artifacts of a legitimate COM+ configuration or application installer"},{"tool":"search","args":{"query":"69.164.192.215","source":"bulk.httplogs"},"purpose":"Challenge f_64524d4b: Determine if 69.164.192.215 is a Linode IP that could be running a legitimate scanner or monitoring service — the finding uses this IP to argue for 'sustained external access' but it could be benign"},{"tool":"search","args":{"query":"archive-security","source":"tsk.filelist"},"purpose":"Challenge f_d2d3e39b: Verify the archived Security log's existence and size — if Archive-Security-2012-03-13 contains pre-incident events, the log clearing happened between 03-13 and 04-03, possibly as part of a normal maintenance window"},{"tool":"audit_evidence_coverage","args":{},"purpose":"AUDIT: Identify indexed evidence sources not cited by any finding — ensures no exculpatory evidence was overlooked"},{"tool":"audit_tool_coverage","args":{},"purpose":"AUDIT: Verify all applicable forensic tools were run against all evidence items — identifies analysis gaps"},{"tool":"deduplicate_findings","args":{"case_id":"SRL-2015","dry_run":true},"purpose":"AUDIT: The consistency analysis identified 34 IOC overlap clusters; findings f_8da2b32c, f_a8c81a78, and f_f636cb35 are synthesis findings that heavily overlap with individual findings and should be evaluated for consolidation"},{"tool":"check_finalize_readiness","args":{},"purpose":"AUDIT: Verify all finalize_report requirements are met before closing the investigation"}],"investigation_questions":["Q-CA1: Could the SysInternals toolkit at C:\\Tools\\SysInternals\\ be a legitimate admin deployment that was cleaned up as part of normal maintenance, rather than attacker tool staging?","Q-CA2: Are the Zeus/Zbot API hooks in services.exe and lsass.exe distinguishable from McAfee HIPS inline hooks (mfehidk.sys), given that McAfee was actively running on all systems? The precooked file was pre-labeled 'zeus-apihooks.txt' — was the analyst anchored to this conclusion?","Q-CA3: Could the blog.gentilkiwi.com domain reference on nromanoff be from legitimate security research browsing rather than actual mimikatz deployment? No mimikatz binary or hash was found on disk.","Q-CA4: Is the Group Policy modification at 2012-04-03 20:38:07 consistent with a normal scheduled GPO refresh (default 90-120 min cycle) rather than a weaponized push? Identical timestamps across workstations are EXPECTED behavior for domain GP updates.","Q-CA5: Could the PSEXESVC.EXE deployments on nromanoff (2012-04-03 21:11-21:19) be legitimate admin activity — e.g., the same team that deployed F-Response forensic tools on 2012-03-20 using PsExec for remote management?","Q-CA6: Is the a.exe beaconing pattern (every ~65 seconds) distinguishable from a legitimate scheduled task, monitoring agent heartbeat, or Windows Update check? Python-based automation tools are common in IT environments.","Q-CA7: Could the empty event logs on nromanoff and nfury be the result of a legitimate log rotation policy (MaxSize reached + AutoBackup disabled) rather than deliberate attacker clearing? The 128 MB allocation on nfury suggests a configured size limit.","Q-CA8: Is the lsass.exe connection to 56.171.18.32 a genuine network connection or a memory scanning artifact? Volatility netscan can produce false positives from deallocated connection pool entries, especially CLOSED connections.","Q-CA9: Could the 'classified document' access (Agents-List-CLASSIFIED-TOP-SECRET at 2012-04-04 15:42) be routine access by the legitimate vibranium user during normal work hours, rather than attacker data staging?","Q-CA10: Are the malfind RWX detections in w3wp.exe, SMSvcHost.exe, and Microsoft.ActiveDirectory.WebServices.exe consistent with normal .NET JIT compilation rather than code injection? All are .NET-based IIS/WCF processes.","Q